 ## Overview
-Y-Bucket Parallelization is a two-pass parallel algorithm for direct volume rendering that processes polygonal representations of isosurfaces or volume slices by grouping them into scanline-based "y-buckets" [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering irregular and multiple grids in software by maximizing spatial coherence and avoiding the computational difficulties associated with ray-casting intersecting cells [S1]. Unlike tile-based parallelization methods that break scanlines and lose coherence, this method parallelizes on a scanline basis to limit duplication [S1].
+Y-Bucket Parallelization is a two-pass parallel algorithm designed for direct volume rendering of irregular or multiple grids, specifically addressing the inefficiencies of ray-casting intersecting cells [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering irregular polygonal data by grouping polygons into scanline-based "y-buckets" to maximize spatial coherence and avoid the duplication inherent in tile-based parallelization [S1]. Unlike [Tiled Parallel Compositing](1192-tiled-parallel-compositing.md) which breaks scanlines, this method parallelizes on a scanline basis to maintain front-to-back visibility order efficiently [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on polygonal data representing volume slices or isosurfaces. The polygons are stored in counter-clockwise order when viewed from outside the grid, allowing the system to easily recognize boundaries and the last polygon in its grid within the visibility order [S1].
+The method processes polygons representing isosurfaces or volume slices. It assumes polygons are stored in counter-clockwise order when viewed from outside the grid, allowing easy recognition of boundaries and visibility order [S1].
 
